 \section{Method}\label{sec:method}
 
+Given a monocular video $\mathcal I=\{\mathbf I_t\}_{t=1}^{T}$, GroundedHuman recovers scene geometry, camera motion, and human meshes in a shared metric world coordinate system. We formulate human--scene grounding as learning \emph{implicit interaction representations}, from which metric calibration and human placement are inferred. These representations encode how scene observations relate to a body's metric extent and current spatial configuration. Human structure determines where and how to query the scene; attention integrates the relevant evidence into a relational representation, and geometric readouts convert it into corrections. The organizing principle is
+\[
+(\mathcal H,\mathcal S)
+\xrightarrow{\text{Query / Attention}}
+\mathbf Z_{\mathrm{int}}
+\xrightarrow{\text{Geometric readout}}
+(\text{Metric},\text{Placement}),
+\]
+where $\mathcal H$ and $\mathcal S$ denote human and scene observations. Interaction is implicit in the learned relation features $\mathbf Z_{\mathrm{int}}$ and the corrections they support, without an intermediate contact-label prediction.
 
