 \begin{abstract}
 
-Reconstructing humans and their surroundings from monocular video requires resolving both metric ambiguity and human-scene interaction. Scene and human estimates may remain inconsistent in scale, while even metrically aligned reconstructions can exhibit floating or penetration due to inaccurate human placement. We introduce GroundedHuman, a framework for monocular dynamic human-scene reconstruction that recovers scene geometry, camera trajectories, and human motion in a shared metric world coordinate system.
-GroundedHuman first establishes a shared metric reference through \textbf{Body-Anchored Metric Calibration}, which combines human-surface scale initialization with body-anchored scene queries and temporal aggregation to calibrate scene depth and camera translation.
-Building on the calibrated geometry, \textbf{Hierarchical Interaction Grounding} reasons over local-to-contextual scene evidence around body regions to infer interaction-aware translation corrections without explicit contact prediction.
-Together, the two stages align scene scale, camera motion, and human placement in a unified metric world, enabling coherent dynamic human--scene reconstruction with improved spatial and contact consistency.
-Extensive experiments evaluate the framework across human motion, scene depth, and camera trajectory estimation, demonstrating improved world-space human motion and qualitatively more consistent human-scene interaction.
+Reconstructing humans and their surroundings from monocular video requires a common metric scale and coherent human placement. These quantities are coupled: a local human--scene discrepancy may arise from either an incorrectly scaled scene or a misplaced body. We introduce GroundedHuman, which infers metric calibration and human placement from learned implicit interaction representations. Human structure determines how to query scene evidence; attention encodes their associations, and geometric readouts convert these relations into corrections. \textbf{Body-Anchored Metric Calibration} initializes scale from human surfaces and uses body-anchored scene queries to encode correspondence for residual calibration of scene depth and camera translation. Within this metric reference, \textbf{Dynamic Interaction Grounding} encodes regional human--environment relations through hierarchical context attention and reads out reliable whole-body translation updates. Each update changes the body's relation to the scene, prompting the interaction representation to be recomputed while preserving pose and shape. This formulation grounds interaction without explicit contact prediction and reconstructs humans, scenes, and camera motion in a shared metric world. Experiments evaluate world-space human motion, scene depth, camera trajectories, and human--scene spatial consistency.
 
 \end{abstract}
 
