 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
 
-We presented GroundedHuman, a framework that connects metric human priors with relative scene geometry to reconstruct humans, scenes, and camera motion from monocular video. Scale calibration establishes a shared metric reference for scene depth and camera translation, while regional translation refinement uses local geometric evidence to adjust human placement without changing pose or shape. Evaluations of world-space motion, scene depth, and local human meshes, together with qualitative comparisons, examine complementary aspects of this reconstruction: body structure, movement, and placement within the environment. The framework makes scene scale and human position explicit calibration variables while retaining the local geometry supplied by pretrained estimators. Future work will explore temporal evidence aggregation and contact constraints to extend this formulation to longer sequences and more complex interactions.
+We presented GroundedHuman, which formulates metric calibration and human placement as geometric readouts of implicit interaction representations. Body-Anchored Scene Query encodes human--scene correspondence to establish a shared metric reference; Hierarchical Context Attention encodes regional relations within that reference to infer placement corrections. Updating placement changes the relations themselves, so Dynamic Interaction Grounding recomputes its representation before the next correction. This connects queries, attention, and geometric updates through a common relation-based formulation while retaining the pose and shape supplied by pretrained estimators. Evaluations examine world-space motion, scene geometry, and human--scene spatial consistency. Extending these interaction representations to temporal evidence and articulated corrections is a direction for future work.
 
 \paragraph{Limitations.}
 GroundedHuman depends on the quality and visibility of the initial human estimates and on the accuracy of scene depth. Biased body shape or root position can affect scale calibration, while occlusion and local depth errors can weaken the evidence for translation refinement. Without reliable human observations, the metric scale is weakly constrained. Preserving pose and shape prevents global alignment errors from being absorbed by local deformation, but also leaves incorrect articulation and body shape unchanged. Translation alone cannot resolve all human--scene contact errors, particularly when these require changes in pose or scene geometry.
